##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    4125 1451  224
                                                                  4125 1451  225
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       4125 1451  226
                                                                  4125 1451  227
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          4125 1451  228
                                                                  4125 1451  229
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   4125 1451  230
                                                                  4125 1451  231
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 4125 1451  232
                                                                  4125 1451  233
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     4125 1451  234
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   4125 1451  235
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             4125 1451  236
                                                                  4125 1451  237
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                4125 1451  238
                                                                  4125 1451  239
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       4125 1451  240
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  4125 1451  241
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   4125 1451  242
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  4125 1451  243
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  4125 1451  244
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 4125 1451  245
to the highest incident energy.                                   4125 1451  246
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:4125 1451  247
                                                                  4125 1451  248
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  4125 1451  249
                                                                  4125 1451  250
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    4125 1451  251
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     4125 1451  252
                                                                  4125 1451  253
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     4125 1451  254
                                                                  4125 1451  255
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    4125 1451  256
total cross section.                                              4125 1451  257
                                                                  4125 1451  258
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            4125 1451  259
                                                                  4125 1451  260
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    4125 1451  261
                                                                  4125 1451  262
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        4125 1451  263
                                                                  4125 1451  264
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  4125 1451  265
                                                                  4125 1451  266
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           4125 1451  267
                                                                  4125 1451  268
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   4125 1451  269
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        4125 1451  270
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     4125 1451  271
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         4125 1451  272
sections.                                                         4125 1451  273
                                                                  4125 1451  274
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 4125 1451  275
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 4125 1451  276
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 4125 1451  277
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 4125 1451  278
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      4125 1451  279
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       4125 1451  280
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              4125 1451  281
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  4125 1451  282
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  4125 1451  283
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  4125 1451  284
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  4125 1451  285
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  4125 1451  286
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       4125 1451  287
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        4125 1451  288
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        4125 1451  289
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        4125 1451  290
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        4125 1451  291
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        4125 1451  292
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        4125 1451  293
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        4125 1451  294
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       4125 1451  295
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        4125 1451  296
                                                                  4125 1451  297
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           4125 1451  298
                                                                  4125 1451  299
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    4125 1451  300
discrete levels.                                                  4125 1451  301
                                                                  4125 1451  302
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         4125 1451  303
                                                                  4125 1451  304
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  4125 1451  305
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   4125 1451  306
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      4125 1451  307
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  4125 1451  308
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      4125 1451  309
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         4125 1451  310
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   4125 1451  311
on an angular grid.                                               4125 1451  312
                                                                  4125 1451  313
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       4125 1451  314
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        4125 1451  315
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         4125 1451  316
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         4125 1451  317
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         4125 1451  318
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        4125 1451  319
                                                                  4125 1451  320
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     4125 1451  321
                                                                  4125 1451  322
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     4125 1451  323
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   4125 1451  324
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  4125 1451  325
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             4125 1451  326
All data are generated with TALYS.                                4125 1451  327
                                                                  4125 1451  328
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   4125 1451  329
                                                                  4125 1451  330
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      4125 1451  331
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   4125 1451  332
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   4125 1451  333
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are4125 1451  334
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   4125 1451  335
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  4125 1451  336
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              4125 1451  337
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 4125 1451  338
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  4125 1451  339
For each residual product:                                        4125 1451  340
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               4125 1451  341
- the recoil                                                      4125 1451  342
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         4125 1451  343
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     4125 1451  344
sections.                                                         4125 1451  345
                                                                  4125 1451  346
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     4125 1451  347
                                                                  4125 1451  348
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  4125 1451  349
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 4125 1451  350
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          4125 1451  351
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       4125 1451  352
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 4125 1451  353
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     4125 1451  354
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  4125 1451  355
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      4125 1451  356
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         4125 1451  357
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             4125 1451  358
                                                                  4125 1451  359
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   4125 1451  360
followed:                                                         4125 1451  361
                                                                  4125 1451  362
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         4125 1451  363
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         4125 1451  364
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         4125 1451  365
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     4125 1451  366
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     4125 1451  367
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     4125 1451  368
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     4125 1451  369
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     4125 1451  370
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     4125 1451  371
                                                                  4125 1451  372
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 4125 1451  373
                                                                  4125 1451  374
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    4125 1451  375
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          4125 1451  376
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     4125 1451  377
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          4125 1451  378
                                                                  4125 1451  379
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  4125 1451  380
                                                                  4125 1451  381
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     4125 1451  382
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    4125 1451  383
as isomer.                                                        4125 1451  384
                                                                  4125 1451  385
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 4125 1451  386
                                                                  4125 1451  387
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        4125 1451  388
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    4125 1451  389
as isomer.                                                        4125 1451  390
                                                                  4125 1451  391
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          4125 1451  392
                                                                  4125 1451  393
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:4125 1451  394
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   4125 1451  395
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   4125 1451  396
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   4125 1451  397
  given                                                           4125 1451  398
                                                                  4125 1451  399
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          4125 1451  400
                                                                  4125 1451  401
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  4125 1451  402
                                                                  4125 1451  403
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 4125 1451  404
                                                                  4125 1451  405
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         4125 1451  406
                                                                  4125 1451  407
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   4125 1451  408
                                                                  4125 1451  409
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        4125 1451  410
and more details are given above in this description. In most     4125 1451  411
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        4125 1451  412
                                                                  4125 1451  413
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 4125 1451  414
                                                                  4125 1451  415
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  4125 1451  416
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     4125 1451  417
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    4125 1451  418
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   4125 1451  419
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  4125 1451  420
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                4125 1451  421
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      4125 1451  422
   same reaction channel between different energies)              4125 1451  423
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      4125 1451  424
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               4125 1451  425
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      4125 1451  426
   different reaction channels for different energies)            4125 1451  427
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        4125 1451  428
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              4125 1451  429
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  4125 1451  430
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       4125 1451  431
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     4125 1451  432
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  4125 1451  433
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     4125 1451  434
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     4125 1451  435
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     4125 1451  436
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   4125 1451  437
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   4125 1451  438
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  4125 1451  439
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   4125 1451  440
                                                                  4125 1451  441
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  4125 1451  442
                                                                  4125 1451  443
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       4125 1451  444
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  4125 1451  445
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       4125 1451  446
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   4125 1451  447
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         4125 1451  448
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  4125 1451  449
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      4125 1451  450
representation is used.                                           4125 1451  451
                                                                  4125 1451  452
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      4125 1451  453
                                                                  4125 1451  454
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  4125 1451  455
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             4125 1451  456
                                                                  4125 1451  457
